<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nebuchadnezzar I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1125–1104 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> I was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in the late twelfth century BCE, of the Second Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. After the Elamite overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassite-dynasty">Kassite dynasty</a>, he led a Babylonian revival. He is best known for his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> that recovered the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, a blow recorded in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-i.jpg" alt="Nebuchadnezzar I" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail,_Kudurru_of_Ritti-Marduk,_from_Sippar,_Iraq,_1125-1104_BCE._British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nebuchadnezzar I, in Babylonian Nabû-kudurrī-uṣur, meaning &quot;Nabû, protect my eldest son&quot; or &quot;Nabû, protect the border&quot;, was the fourth king of the Second Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. He ruled for 22 years according to the Babylonian King List C and was the most prominent monarch of the dynasty. He succeeded his father, Ninurta-nādin-šumi, and was followed in turn by his son Enlil-nādin-apli, his brother Marduk-nādin-aḫḫē and then his nephew Marduk-šāpik-zēri. He is unrelated to the later Nabû-kudurrī-uṣur II, known by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> form of the name, and does not appear in the Bible. Applying the name Nebuchadnezzar to the earlier king is therefore anachronistic but not inappropriate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The length of his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and the number of his campaigns are not known. A later literary tradition says an invasion was thwarted when plague struck his army. A kudurru of his reign records a successful raid, made with the Kassite chieftain Šitti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and launched from Dēr in the hottest summer month, in which he routed the Elamite king Ḫulteludiš-Inšušinak on the banks of the river Ulaya. No source records a sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, but another kudurru says he retrieved the statue of Marduk, called Bēl, and that of the goddess Il-āliya. The victory was marked in hymns and poetry, including a poem on the recovery of Marduk's statue.</p><p>The Synchronistic History relates an agreement with the Assyrian king Aššur-rēša-iši I and two campaigns against the border fortresses of Zanqi and Idi, undertaken in breach of it. The first ended when Aššur-rēša-iši's main force arrived and Nebuchadnezzar burned his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a> and fled; in the second the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> apparently won and captured the Babylonian field marshal Karaštu. A kudurru also calls him conqueror of the Amorite lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Building inscriptions record his work on the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and bricks from the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> carry his name. Only three economic texts, three kudurrus and a stone memorial tablet survive as contemporary records; one kudurru records a land grant at Nippur. Several literary texts are thought to come from his age, in Sumerian and Akkadian, and the sage Šaggil-kīnam-ubbib is said to have served under him.</p><p>Lambert has suggested that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> was raised to the head of the pantheon in his reign and that the Enûma Eliš was possibly composed then, though some historians claim an origin in the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassite-dynasty">Kassite dynasty</a>. It has also been suggested that the return of Marduk's statue was a literary tradition of the first millennium BCE rather than a historical record.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nebuchadnezzar I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The defeat of the Kassite dynasty in the mid-twelfth century BCE ended more than four centuries of Kassite rule and created the conditions for the Babylonian revival under <b>Nebuchadnezzar I</b>, who later recovered the statue of Marduk from Susa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…was once thought to have been composed under Hammurabi, but most scholars now date it to the Second Dynasty of Isin, when Marduk began to be called king of the gods after <b>Nebuchadnezzar I</b> brought his statue back from Elam; a late Kassite date has also been proposed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/enuma-elis">Enūma Eliš</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1110 BCE <b>Nebuchadnezzar I</b> of the Second Dynasty of Isin defeated Hutelutush-Inshushinak and recovered the statue of Marduk.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-elamite-kingdom">Middle Elamite kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty's high point was <b>Nebuchadnezzar I</b> (c. 1125 to 1104 BCE), who defeated Hutelutush-Inshushinak of the Middle Elamite kingdom and returned the statue of Marduk, an event celebrated in literary texts and possibly linked to the rise of Marduk as king of the gods in the Enuma…</blockquote><a href="/state/second-dynasty-of-isin">Second Dynasty of Isin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-1115" aria-label="Babylon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5690 981 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Nebuchadnezzar I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6140 1251h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-1115">What was here in 1115 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
